nds):5\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0\n\nPosition7 (millions):5\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):5\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):7\n\nPlus the billions digit 1.\n\nSo constructing the number:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:7,\n\nTen millions:5,\n\nMillions:5,\n\nHundred thousands:0,\n\nTen thousands:7,\n\nThousands:5,\n\nHundreds:8,\n\nTens:1,\n\nOnes:4.\n\nSo the number is 1,755,078,514.\n\nBut in the other method, splitting into parts, I got 1,755,075,814. The difference is in the thousands and hundreds places. Let me see:\n\nThe thousands digit in the digit-by-digit is 5, and the hundreds is 8. In the other method, when adding 86,427 + 989,387:\n\nLet me compute that again:\n\n86,427 + 989,387.\n\nAdding the two:\n\n86,427\n\n+989,387\n\n=?\n\nLet's add:\n\nStart from the right:\n\nOnes:7+7=14 \u21924, carry1.\n\nTens:2+8=10 +1=11 \u21921, carry1.\n\nHundreds:4+3=7 +1=8 \u21928, carry0.\n\nThousands:6+9=15 \u21925, carry1.\n\nTen thousands:8+8=16 +1=17 \u21927, carry1.\n\nHundred thousands:0 (from 86,427) +9 (from 989,387) =9 +1=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nMillions:0 (since 86k has no millions) +0 (since 989k also has none) plus carry1 \u21921.\n\nWait, wait:\n\nWait 86,427 is written as 000086427, and 989,387 is 00989387. Wait, adding them:\n\nActually, 86,427 is 0086427 (if we consider up to hundred thousands), and 989,387 is 0989387.\n\nWait, but adding 86,427 (which is 0086427) and 989,387 (which is 0989387):\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nPosition1 (ones):7+7=14 \u21924, carry1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):2+8=10 +1=11 \u21921, carry1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):4+3=7 +1=8 \u21928, carry0.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):6+9=15 \u21925, carry1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):8+8=16 +1=17 \u21927, carry1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0+9=9 +1=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):0+0 +1=1 \u21921.\n\nSo the total here is 1,075,814.\n\nTherefore, adding that to 1,754,000,000 gives 1,755,075,814.\n\nHmm, so now there's a discrepancy between the two methods. Which is correct?\n\nWait in the digit-by-digit addition, the thousands digit was 5 and hundreds 8, which matches the 86,427 + 989,387 result (the thousands digit in 86,427 is 6, in 989,387 is 9 \u2192 15, so 5 with carryover, leading to 5 in thousands and the 10 from the ten thousands etc. So the 86,427 + 989,387 part gives 1,075,814, which has:\n\nDigits:\n\n1,075,814 \u2192 millions digit 1, hundred thousands 0, ten thousands 7, thousands 5, hundreds 8, tens 1, ones 4. Exactly what the digit-by-digit method gave for those digits.\n\nWait but then when adding to the 1,754,000,000, that should be 1,754,000,000 +1,075,814 = 1,755,075,814. But according to the digit-by-digit total, the number is 1,755,078,514. Wait, so where is the mistake?\n\nWait, in the digit-by-digit total, the millions digit was 5, but in the split method, the millions digit is 5 (from the first number's millions digit 1 plus second's 3 plus carryover 1 gives 5), which is correct. The hundred thousands digit is 0 (from 0+9+1 carryover), which is correct. The ten thousands digit is 7 (from 8+8+1 carryover). The thousands digit is 5 (from 6+9), hundreds is 8 (from 4+3+1 carryover). So the number should be:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n7 (hundred millions),\n\n5 (ten millions),\n\n5 (millions),\n\n0 (hundred thousands),\n\n7 (ten thousands),\n\n5 (thousands),\n\n8 (hundreds),\n\n1 (tens),\n\n4 (ones).\n\nWhich is 1,755,078,514.\n\nBut according to the split method, the total is 1,755,075,814. Wait, the difference is in the thousands and ten thousands places?\n\nWait let me write both numbers:\n\nDigit-by-digit: 1,755,078,514.\n\nSplit method: 1,755,075,814.\n\nWait, no, actually in the split method, the 1,075,814 added to 1,754,000,000 gives:\n\n1,754,000,000\n\n+   1,075,814\n\n=1,755,075,814.\n\nBut according to digit-by-digit, it's 1,755,078,514.\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error in the digit-by-digit addition.\n\nWait let me check the ten thousands place again.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands